routes.brief:
Build on Heights, rational points and obstructions (HeightsRationalPointsAndObstructions:RP.2), which owns adelic points, local invariants and the Brauer–Manin pairing. The new theory is the p-adic evaluation of Brauer classes of p-power order on smooth models: Bright–Newton, Evaluating the wild Brauer group, Invent. Math. 234 (2023). It imports the Swan-conductor theory from the companion candidate LocalFieldsPartIIKatoSwanConductors, "Local fields and ramification, Part II: Kato's Swan conductors for imperfect residue fields", which must be built first.

The final theorems, exactly as in the paper (setting: k/Q_p finite, X/k smooth geometrically irreducible, 𝒳/O_k a smooth model, not necessarily proper, with geometrically irreducible special fibre Y):
- Theorem A:
  (1) Ev_{−2} Br X = {A ∈ fil_0 : ∂A = 0};
  (2) Ev_{−1} Br X = {A ∈ fil_0 : ∂A ∈ H¹(𝔽, Q/Z)};
  (3) Ev_0 Br X = fil_0 Br X;
  (4) for n ≥ 1, Ev_n Br X = {A ∈ fil_{n+1} Br X : rsw_{n+1}(A) ∈ [Ω²_F, 0]_{π,n+1}}.
  The same holds with unramified k′/k only (Corollaries 9.4 and 9.7).
- Theorem 8.1 (containing Theorem B), for A ∈ fil_n Br X with rsw_n(A) = [α, β]_{π,n}:
  (1) α and β are regular at P₀;
  (2) inv A(Q) = inv A(P) + (1/p)Tr_{𝔽/𝔽_p} β_{P₀}(v_n(P, Q)) for Q ∈ B(P, n);
  (3) the bilinear formula with γ when β = 0 and n > 2, and its refinement for 1 ≤ s < n/2 under the stated bound n < e′ + 2 + te;
  (4) if β = 0 and α_{P₀} ≠ 0, then p distinct values on some B(Q, n−1) with Q ∈ B(P, 1);
  (5) surjectivity onto Br k[p^{t+1}] under the stated conditions.
- Corollary 9.8: behaviour of Ev_n under base change. When α = 0, use β′ ≠ 0 (sourceIssue E4).
- Theorem C (potential failure of weak approximation at good ordinary primes when H⁰(V, Ω²) ≠ 0), and Theorem D (evaluation constant outside the explicit set S).
- Questions 1.3–1.5, recorded as questions. Record Wittenberg's Question 1.4 as answered only for varieties with a prime of good ordinary reduction (sourceIssue E7).

Cover, with API and tests:
- the evaluation filtration and p-adic discs;
- Proposition 3.1, Corollary 3.2, Lemmas 3.3, 3.5, 3.6 and Corollary 3.7 (Br 𝒳 = ker ∂; with Theorem A(1) this recovers Saito–Sato for smooth 𝒳);
- the log-residue exact sequence and the isomorphisms ψ, φ, and Lemma 5.1;
- Lemmas 6.1 and 6.2 (in 6.1(3) the basis is X_i² d(X_j/X_i), sourceIssue E1);
- Lemmas 7.1 and 7.3 (tangent vectors, base change, strict transforms);
- the blowup induction of §8: Lemmas 8.3, 8.4, 8.6–8.11, including the corestricted cyclic algebras of Lemma 8.11;
- §9: Lemmas 9.1–9.5 (9.2 with res A ∈ fil_{n+1}, E2; 9.5 without the stray 'for every r ≥ 1', E3) and Corollaries 9.4, 9.7, 9.8;
- §11: Lemmas 11.1–11.3, Bloch–Kato's ordinary-reduction input (gr⁰ H²(V̄_𝔭, Q_p) ≠ 0), Colliot-Thélène–Skorobogatov's Proposition 2.4 for the prime-to-p part, and Remarks 1.6 and 11.5 (K3 surfaces).
  Remark 1.6(1) needs Fontaine–Messing's mod-p comparison and Deligne–Illusie degeneration, which the atlas does not plan. Keep it as an required cited statement unless an owner appears; no proof depends on it.

Import, never re-plan:
- fil_n, λ_π, δ_r, rsw_n, ∂, Kato's Theorems 7.1 and 8.1, Lemmas 2.16–2.19, Lemma 3.4, fil~_n and Bloch–Kato's vanishing-cycle filtration: LocalFieldsPartIIKatoSwanConductors;
- the local invariant, the residue isomorphism Br k ≅ H¹(𝔽, Q/Z) and restriction: Class field theory (tauceti:TauCetiRoadmap/ClassFieldTheory), Layer 5;
- the Brauer–Manin pairing and the one-place obstruction test: RP.2 (this paper is a source there);
- étale cohomology, proper base change, Hochschild–Serre, Brauer purity and Lemma 11.4: SchemeAndStackFoundations:SF.2;
- Hensel lifting of points of smooth O_k-schemes: SF.4;
- Lang–Weil: WeilConjectures:WC.5;
- semicontinuity: the Jacobian Challenge (tauceti:TauCetiRoadmap/JacobianChallenge), Layer C;
- de Rham–Witt complexes and the Cartier operator: CrystallineCohomology:CR.4 and DerivedDeRhamCohomology:DD.3.
Hodge symmetry h^{1,0} = h^{0,1} in characteristic 0 (used in Theorem D) has no located owner: flag it to the maintainer, or prove h⁰(Ω¹) = h¹(O) through the Picard and Albanese varieties.

Tests:
- a conic bundle or cyclic algebra with β_{P₀} ≠ 0, checking the trace formula;
- p = 2 with n = 2 (Lemma 8.10);
- a class with β = 0 and α_{P₀} ≠ 0 over a surface;
- base change by a ramified extension (Corollary 9.8);
- a K3 surface over Q with S = {∞, bad primes, 2}. REVIEW GATE: not approved for design. Questions 1.3–1.5 are problem definitions, not theorem goals. State Theorem 8.1(3),(5) in full, repair E10–E12 including the original-model descent obligation, and close the Fontaine–Messing/Deligne–Illusie, blowup geometry, Picard and ordinary-vanishing-cycle dependencies. Theorem C retains its good-ordinary-reduction hypothesis. The 2025 sequel is separate literature, not a proof supplied by this paper.
